Yeah, sure. That's a great question.

00:01:01.051 --> 00:01:05.752
So let me just mention&nbsp;
the common steps of model training.

00:01:05.752 --> 00:01:08.471
So first, you need to prep your data,

00:01:08.839 --> 00:01:12.965
your data set for&nbsp;training.

00:01:13.139 --> 00:01:17.760
And, ideally you also want to do it for testing.

00:01:17.876 --> 00:01:23.367
And then, the other steps is you're&nbsp;
going to build your model.

00:01:25.498 --> 00:01:28.464
And you're going to train it.

00:01:29.045 --> 00:01:34.064
And as I mentioned, you're going&nbsp;to test.

00:01:34.471 --> 00:01:37.650
Okay. So those look like some
pretty straightforward features.

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:41.264
Why don't you tell me&nbsp;about the first one? 
What do you mean by prepping the data?

00:01:41.419 --> 00:01:46.740
Right. So, the data says are you going to use&nbsp;
for your model, maybe small as you're learning it,

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:52.863
but for larger models, these data sets can&nbsp;
be huge--10 terabytes, petabytes wide.

00:01:52.863 --> 00:01:58.700
So how do you use this data
to train your model and&nbsp;then test it?

00:01:59.087 --> 00:02:02.091
So PyTorch provides you two things here.

00:02:02.207 --> 00:02:14.424
Data sets and data loader classes that help&nbsp;
you to easily feed this data for your training and testing.

00:02:14.579 --> 00:02:17.200
Okay. How does this help me?
Does it speed things up?

00:02:17.336 --> 00:02:22.847
Well, that's a good question. So, it helps you to download 
the data to make it&nbsp;accessible for your training and testing.

00:02:22.847 --> 00:02:30.837
And this data loader, it provides you iterator over this&nbsp;
data so that you can use them to train in a batch.

00:02:30.837 --> 00:02:37.734
Because you're not going to just feed one data&nbsp;
at a time. You're going to train using the batch sizes that you want.

00:02:37.734 --> 00:02:41.136
It also provides you other&nbsp;things like shuffling the data.

00:02:41.233 --> 00:02:49.549
You don't want to just feed the data in an order so that your&nbsp;model, 
it's only memorizing the data versus versus it's learning.

00:02:49.666 --> 00:02:53.236
So this will shuffle for you as well.&nbsp;
It has other features as well.

00:02:53.372 --> 00:02:56.682
Very nice. Well, it also helps you to build models? 

00:02:56.818 --> 00:03:01.200
Absolutely. So,&nbsp;once you think you're ready with your preparation

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:05.600
using PyTorch because it takes you all the takes&nbsp;
care of all the complexity, the next step would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:12.120
be and building the model to define your models&nbsp;
and for that what you need is layers because it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:17.000
a deep learning, it's made of multiple layers.&nbsp;
So you need different layers like linear layer or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:23.400
combination layer. And there are many others that&nbsp;
are provided by partners to you. And that are also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:31.480
things like, besides layers, that are activation&nbsp;
functions that you'll be using to add nonlinearity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:36.760
to your model-- that's also provided to you by&nbsp;
PyTorch. So you don't have to do anything but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:38.824
just to call those functions.  

00:03:38.824 --> 00:03:40.630
What do you&nbsp;mean by nonlinearity?

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:46.800
So, in general,&nbsp;when you train the model and then-- it's a&nbsp;
mathematical term, right, linear as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:51.680
but it will if you don't get nonlinear, you&nbsp;
basically just get like a one straight line.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.680 --> 00:04:00.200
And in real life, not everything is just&nbsp;
changing in X will be same as changing your Y&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:08.200
output. So it adds you that nonlinearity for&nbsp;
you. And the next step would be training and there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:12.648
basically I can talk more about the training side, Brad.&nbsp;

00:04:12.648 --> 00:04:15.920
Well, so tell me about features-- 
what&nbsp;&nbsp;does it do to help you train? 

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:19.480
So training will&nbsp;require to use the loss function. And loss function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:27.480
is basically to find out the loss that you going&nbsp;
to have. As when you run this model like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:34.520
a forward pass from the input and you get some&nbsp;
output. Well, I'm not going to have the correct output&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:39.120
every time magically, there's no magic there. So&nbsp;
you can have lots of parameters in between, you&nbsp;are

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:43.000
just going to randomize them in the beginning,&nbsp;
you got some output, but then you're going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:47.728
have a loss function to calculate the loss from&nbsp;
the desired output.

00:04:47.728 --> 00:04:55.880
So your want your model to reach a certain expectation. 
And typically during&nbsp;the training process that model's falling short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:58.790
and you're seeing how much it's falling short&nbsp;
from where you want it to be.

00:04:58.790 --> 00:05:04.720
Exactly. So that loss functions, there are multiple loss
functions&nbsp;and PyTorch provides it to you again. You again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:13.560
you call them according to your need for&nbsp;
the model. Once you have the loss function used,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:20.680
the next big thing is finding the gradient of this&nbsp;
loss with regards to your parameters. So PyTorch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:32.560
provides the backward propagation for you, or,&nbsp;
auto-grade features. That is by far one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:37.870
most popular feature of PyTorch, that it will&nbsp;
calculate the gradient for you. 

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:46.200
So if we all&nbsp;think back from our calculus days, gradients are&nbsp;
this piece that helps you to tweak and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:49.554
get the model the way you want it and it's got&nbsp;
it built-in for you. 

00:05:49.554 --> 00:05:55.400
Exactly. So once you got&nbsp;the gradient, you basically
run the optimizer&nbsp;function just to step over, which is again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.400 --> 00:06:00.080
provided by PyTorch to you. And like you exactly&nbsp;
said, you're going to tweak the parameters, you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:07.240
going to optimize it to reach to a level in a&nbsp;
number of iterations. So that you basically define&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:12.280
those iterations. But the number of iterations&nbsp;
you're going to reach to a level where we're like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:19.120
you know what, that should be enough training.&nbsp;
I do like 3x, 5x iterations, and at that point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:22.216
you are ready to test it. 

