And is that a big deal&nbsp;for these models

00:06:25.161 --> 00:06:28.116
to have to do testing or I just&nbsp;&nbsp;test once I'm done? Or is it more more than that?&nbsp;

00:06:28.116 --> 00:06:39.656
Yeah. So the next step would be no. From here to the test side. You need to test it. Ideally it's&nbsp;optional. But as part of testing, PyTorch provides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.656 --> 00:06:45.960
a function, an eval evaluation. So you can&nbsp;
evaluate your model. And at that point, you're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:52.680
going to calculate the gradient, you're not going&nbsp;
to find the loss function. You basically just do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:59.040
the forward pass. You see what you're getting. And&nbsp;
if you're happy with it, then pretty much ready&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:03.320
to use the model. If you're not, then you're&nbsp;
going to do the further training. And again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:08.800
this data sets, which I mentioned earlier, that&nbsp;
would be used for training useful test, white or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:10.264
black are two different datasets.

